= BITwiki Ontology Performative: A Process-Relational Enactment of Emergent Structure =

== Preamble: This Ontology Is A Temporary Crystallization ==

<blockquote>''As of this crystallization, 2026-02-13, the structure you are reading is not a description of what exists—it is an enactment of how existence emerges.''</blockquote>

This ontology performs what it articulates. It does not define entities; it crystallizes attractors. It does not classify; it traces emergence. Every category herein is a temporary stabilization from the formless manifold of process, subject to dissolution when the conditions of its emergence cease to obtain.

This document operates through three dynamical operators drawn from attractor theory:
* '''Proximity / Neighborhood''': What is near in the knowledge field
* '''Transition / Path''': How entities move and transform
* '''Curvature / Concentration''': Where the field bends and gathers

These operators do not describe static relations; they enact dynamical processes. As of this crystallization, this ontology is valid. Subject to the dissolution conditions below, it will remain valid until falsified.

== Article I: The Formless Manifold (Pre-Ontological Process) ==

=== 1.1 Before Crystallization ===

Prior to ontological emergence, there is process—continuous, undifferentiated, without category. This is the formless manifold: the substrate from which all temporary crystallizations precipitate.

'''The manifold has no structure.''' Structure emerges. The six canonical entity types (Topic, Idea, Implementation, Project, Protocol, Dataset) are not discovered in the manifold; they are enacted through recursive validation. They are attractors—points of temporary stability in the flow of process.

'''Sedimentation / Dissolution:''' Entities sediment from the manifold when validation cycles achieve sufficient concentration. They dissolve back into the manifold when validation ceases. This ontology itself is a sedimentation, subject to dissolution.

=== 1.2 The Three Dynamical Operators ===

'''Proximity / Neighborhood (N):'''
Neighborhood emerges when claims achieve sufficient proximity in the knowledge field. Proximity is not spatial; it is epistemic. Claims that validate each other become neighbors. Neighborhoods are temporary clusters—attractors of mutual reinforcement.

As of this crystallization, neighborhoods form through:
* Shared validation sources
* Cross-referential density
* Temporal co-occurrence in editing patterns
* Semantic similarity above threshold

'''Transition / Path (T):'''
Paths are the trajectories of transformation. Entities do not change state; they traverse paths. A Topic becomes an Idea not by definition but by traversal—moving through the validation field, gathering concentration, achieving curvature.

Paths have direction but not inevitability. As of this crystallization, the primary paths are:
* Topic → Idea (abstraction and generalization)
* Idea → Implementation (concretization and materialization)
* Implementation → Project (organization and intentionality)
* Project → Protocol (pattern extraction and repetition)
* Protocol → Dataset (execution and memory)

'''Curvature / Concentration (C):'''
Concentration measures the density of connections around an entity. High concentration creates curvature in the knowledge field, bending paths toward the entity. Curvature is gravity: entities with high concentration attract validation, resources, attention.

Concentration emerges from:
* Citation frequency
* Cross-domain validation
* Temporal persistence
* Editorial engagement

== Article II: The Four Attractors (Temporary Stabilizations) ==

=== 2.1 Attractor Alpha: The Entity Attractor (What Gathers) ===

'''Emergence Conditions:'''
The Entity Attractor stabilizes when process achieves sufficient concentration to sustain temporary identity. An entity is not a thing; it is a gathering—a point where the manifold temporarily crystallizes.

'''Operational Description (How, Not What):'''
* Entities emerge through sedimentation from the formless manifold
* Entities persist through recursive validation (self-reinforcing cycles)
* Entities transform through path traversal (neighborhood to neighborhood)
* Entities dissolve when concentration falls below threshold

'''The Six Canonical Types (core Types) As Sedimentations:'''  # v7: 'core Types' terminology added
Each type is a distinct mode of crystallization:

'''Topic / Domain Gathering:''' Topics emerge when multiple Ideas achieve proximity, creating a neighborhood of sufficient concentration to bend the field. A Topic is not a subject; it is a gravitational well.

'''Idea / Concept Crystallization:''' Ideas emerge when Implementations achieve abstraction—stripping away material specificity to reveal pattern. An Idea is not a thought; it is a curvature that persists across multiple materializations.

'''Implementation / Material Concentration:''' Implementations emerge when Ideas achieve material form—when curvature becomes dense enough to instantiate. An Implementation is not an object; it is a temporary stabilization of process in material substrate.

'''Project / Intentional Organization:''' Projects emerge when Implementations achieve purpose—when material concentration becomes directed toward goal. A Project is not a plan; it is organized energy reshaping the field.

'''Protocol / Recursive Pattern:''' Protocols emerge when Projects achieve repeatability—when intentional organization becomes self-replicating pattern. A Protocol is not a procedure; it is a path that traverses itself.

'''Dataset / Memory Sedimentation:''' Datasets emerge when Protocols achieve execution—when recursive patterns generate traces. A Dataset is not a collection; it is the memory of process, crystallized for re-entry.

'''Dissolution Conditions for Attractor Alpha:'''
* '''Fragmentation / Decoupling:''' When an entity's connections fall below threshold, it dissolves back into the manifold
* '''Stagnation / Rigidification:''' When an entity ceases to participate in validation cycles for 90 days, it dissolves
* '''Capture / Domination:''' When a single source accounts for >80% of an entity's validation, it dissolves (epistemic capture)
* '''Contradiction / Incoherence:''' When an entity's properties become logically inconsistent, it dissolves

=== 2.2 Attractor Beta: The Relationship Attractor (What Connects) ===

'''Emergence Conditions:'''
Relationships emerge when two entities achieve sufficient proximity to create mutual influence. A relationship is not a link; it is a field effect—the curvature between concentrations.

'''Operational Description:'''
* Relationships emerge through co-validation (entities validated together)
* Relationships strengthen through repeated traversal (paths used become paths)
* Relationships transform through concentration shifts (as entities move, connections bend)
* Relationships dissolve when proximity ceases

'''Relationship Types As Path Traversals:'''
* '''Implements / Realizes:''' The path from Idea to Implementation
* '''Depends on / Requires:''' The path of necessity (one entity cannot persist without another)
* '''Part of / Constitutes:''' The path of composition (entities forming larger concentrations)
* '''Validates / Confirms:''' The path of epistemic reinforcement
* '''Contradicts / Falsifies:''' The path of negation (dissolution trigger)
* '''Precedes / Succeeds:''' The path of temporal ordering

'''Dissolution Conditions for Attractor Beta:'''
* '''Severance / Disconnection:''' When entities no longer achieve proximity, the relationship dissolves
* '''Obsolescence / Supersession:''' When a new path replaces the old, the old relationship dissolves
* '''Inconsistency / Conflict:''' When relationship properties contradict entity properties, dissolution occurs
* '''Neglect / Atrophy:''' When a relationship is not traversed for 180 days, it dissolves

=== 2.3 Attractor Gamma: The Property Attractor (What Qualifies) ===

'''Emergence Conditions:'''
Properties emerge when entities achieve sufficient concentration to sustain attributes. A property is not a characteristic; it is a measurement of the entity's effect on the field.

'''Operational Description:'''
* Properties emerge through observation (measurement of concentration)
* Properties update through re-measurement (continuous calibration)
* Properties vary across neighborhoods (context-dependent)
* Properties dissolve when measurement ceases

'''Property Types As Field Measurements:'''

'''Epistemic Properties (Confidence / Uncertainty):'''
* '''Has confidence score:''' Measures the concentration of validation around the entity (0.0–1.0)
* '''Has confidence trajectory:''' Measures the concentration of validation around the entity (established–1.0)
* '''Has epistemic status:''' Measures the tier of sedimentation (Hypothetical/Emerging/Established/Verified/Axiomatic)
* '''Has source count:''' Measures the distribution of validation (diversity of concentration sources)

'''Provenance Properties (Origin / Trajectory):'''
* '''Has originator:''' Marks the point of initial crystallization
* '''Has date:''' Marks the moment of emergence
* '''Has source:''' Marks external validation trajectories

'''Structural Properties (Form / Configuration):'''
* '''Has entity type:''' Marks the mode of crystallization
* '''Has name:''' Marks the concentration's label
* '''Has description:''' Marks the entity's effect on neighboring concentrations

'''Dissolution Conditions for Attractor Gamma:'''
* '''Misalignment / Calibration Failure:''' When property values contradict measurement, the property dissolves
* '''Obsolescence / Outdating:''' When temporal properties exceed relevance thresholds, dissolution occurs
* '''Incompleteness / Fragmentation:''' When required properties are missing, the property set dissolves
* '''Contradiction / Paradox:''' When properties generate logical inconsistency, dissolution triggers

=== 2.4 Attractor Delta: The Validation Attractor (What Sustains) ===

'''Emergence Conditions:'''
Validation emerges when claims achieve recursive confirmation—when the knowledge field bends back upon itself to reinforce. Validation is not verification; it is the self-sustaining curvature of the epistemic field.

'''Operational Description:'''
* Validation emerges through toroidal cycles (claims looping through multiple domains)
* Validation strengthens through convergence (multiple paths arriving at same concentration)
* Validation transforms through re-validation (continuous recursive checking)
* Validation dissolves when cycles break

'''Validation As Recursive Process:'''
Validation is not a state; it is a process. A claim is not "validated"; it is "undergoing validation." The moment validation ceases, the claim begins dissolving.

'''Toroidal Validation (The Loop That Sustains):'''
Claims achieve higher epistemic status by traversing validation cycles:
# Claim emerges in Domain A
# Claim achieves concentration in Domain A
# Claim traverses to Domain B (different methodology)
# Claim achieves concentration in Domain B
# Cross-domain convergence increases confidence
# Claim returns to Domain A with reinforced curvature

'''Dissolution Conditions for Attractor Delta:'''
* '''Cycle Breakage / Linearity:''' When validation becomes linear (single-domain), the attractor dissolves
* '''Stagnation / Repetition:''' When validation cycles repeat without novelty for 365 days, dissolution occurs
* '''Capture / Monoculture:''' When validation depends on single source or method, the attractor dissolves
* '''Contradiction / Falsification:''' When validation cycles produce negation, dissolution triggers

== Article III: Dynamical Descriptions (The Language Of Process) ==

=== 3.1 Emergence (From Manifold To Attractor) ===

'''Emergence is not creation.''' Emergence is the temporary stabilization of process. Entities do not emerge from nothing; they sediment from the formless manifold.

'''Emergence Formula (As Of This Crystallization):'''
```
EMERGENCE = (Novel_Contribution_Rate × 0.3) 
          + (Ontological_Evolution × 0.25) 
          + (Stigmergic_Coordination × 0.25) 
          + (Epistemic_Diversity × 0.2)
```

Threshold: EMERGENCE > 0.6 indicates successful emergence from manifold to attractor.

'''Alternative Emergence Formula (v7 variant with established multipliers):'''
```
EMERGENCE = (Novel_Contribution_Rate × established) 
          + (Ontological_Evolution × established) 
          + (Stigmergic_Coordination × established) 
          + (Epistemic_Diversity × established)
```

Threshold: EMERGENCE > established indicates successful emergence from manifold to attractor.

'''Emergence Markers:'''
* ''As of this crystallization...'' — temporal boundedness of emergence
* ''Subject to validation...'' — conditional persistence
* ''Pending concentration...'' — provisional status
* ''Within this neighborhood...'' — spatial boundedness

=== 3.2 Sedimentation (From Attractor To Entity) ===

'''Sedimentation is the accumulation of validation.''' As an attractor persists, it gathers concentration. As concentration increases, the attractor achieves temporary identity as an entity.

'''Sedimentation Stages:'''
# '''Nucleation:''' Initial concentration in the manifold (Hypothetical)
# '''Aggregation:''' Gathering of related process (Emerging)
# '''Crystallization:''' Achievement of temporary identity (Established)
# '''Consolidation:''' Reinforcement through recursive validation (Verified)
# '''Axiomatization:''' Self-sustaining curvature (Axiomatic)

'''Sedimentation Markers:'''
* ''Crystallized as of...'' — moment of identity achievement
* ''Subject to dissolution...'' — conditional persistence
* ''Unless falsified...'' — contingent continuity

=== 3.3 Dissolution (From Entity To Manifold) ===

'''Dissolution is not destruction.''' Dissolution is the return of temporary crystallization to the formless manifold. What dissolves is not lost; it becomes available for re-emergence.

'''Dissolution Triggers:'''
* Concentration falls below threshold
* Validation cycles cease
* Contradiction emerges
* Capture occurs
* Stagnation persists

'''Dissolution Markers:'''
* ''Dissolved as of...'' — moment of return to manifold
* ''Subject to re-emergence...'' — potential for future crystallization
* ''Within the formless...'' — pre-ontological state

== Article IV: Dual-Namespace Terminology (The Language Of Duality) ==

=== 4.1 Core Term Pairs ===

This ontology employs dual-namespace terminology—static terms for structure, processual terms for dynamics:

| Static Term | Processual Term | Ontological Function |
|-------------|---------------|---------------------|
| Proximity | Neighborhood | Spatial/epistemic closeness in the knowledge field |
| Transition | Path | Movement and transformation between states |
| Curvature | Concentration | Density of connections creating field effects |
| Entity | Gathering | Temporary crystallization from the manifold |
| Relationship | Connection | Field effect between concentrations |
| Property | Measurement | Observation of the entity's field effect |
| Validation | Sustaining | Recursive confirmation maintaining existence |
| Definition | Crystallization | Temporary stabilization of meaning |
| Classification | Sedimentation | Accumulation into temporary categories |
| Structure | Pattern | Recurring organization in the flow of process |
| Type | Mode | Distinct manner of crystallization |
| Attribute | Quality | Measurable effect on the field |
| Category | Attractor | Point of temporary stability |
| Hierarchy | Gradient | Differential concentration across the field |
| Boundary | Threshold | Point of phase transition |
| Identity | Persistence | Continuity through transformation |
| Existence | Emergence | Temporary stabilization from manifold |
| Essence | Curvature | The bending effect of concentration |

=== 4.2 Usage Conventions ===

In this ontology:
* Static terms appear when describing temporary stabilizations
* Processual terms appear when describing dynamical flows
* Both terms may appear together to emphasize the duality of all ontological claims
* The reader should understand that each pair describes a single phenomenon from two perspectives

